n\n\\[\nx = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the \\(x\\)-intercept \\(a\\) is \\(4\\).\n\n### Step 2: Find the \\(y\\)-intercept \\(b\\)\nThe \\(y\\)-intercept occurs when \\(x = 0\\). Substitute \\(x = 0\\) into the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{0}{4} + \\frac{y}{12} = 1\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y}{12} = 1\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\ny = 12\n\\]\n\nThus, the \\(y\\)-intercept \\(b\\) is \\(12\\).\n\n### Step 3: Find the slope \\(m\\)\nTo find the slope, we need to rewrite the equation in the slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\). Start with the given equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{4} + \\frac{y}{12} = 1\n\\]\n\nMultiply every term by 12 to clear the fractions:\n\n\\[\n12 \\cdot \\frac{x}{4} + 12 \\cdot \\frac{y}{12} = 12 \\cdot 1\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\[\n3x + y = 12\n\\]\n\nRearrange to solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\ny = -3x + 12\n\\]\n\nFrom this form, we can see that the slope \\(m\\) is \\(-3\\).\n\n### Step 4: Calculate \\(a + b + m\\)\nNow, we sum the values of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(m\\):\n\n\\[\na + b + m = 4 + 12 - 3\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\[\n4 + 12 - 3 = 13\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a + b + m\\) is \\(\\boxed{13}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:450"}
